WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.440
All right. Hello, everybody, and welcome. So we at Observable are so excited to host

00:13.440 --> 00:20.520
this discussion on how data journalists approach storytelling with data. So in a moment I will

00:20.520 --> 00:26.240
be introducing our panelists, but before I do, I just want to go through some quick logistics.

00:26.240 --> 00:30.760
So first of all, one of our panelists, Jackie Schrag, unfortunately will not be able to

00:30.760 --> 00:37.560
participate today, but we do hope she can join us in a future session. Now, what to

00:37.560 --> 00:44.080
expect today. So in just a moment I'll introduce our panelists and we will dive into our conversation,

00:44.080 --> 00:49.440
which we expect to run for about 30 minutes. After we wrap up the roundtable, we're going

00:49.440 --> 00:54.920
to have about 10 minutes of Q&A, and you're welcome to either add your questions in the

00:54.920 --> 01:01.560
chat during the roundtable at any point, or you can wait until the Q&A to do so. And we'll try to

01:01.560 --> 01:06.920
get through as many questions as we can in that time. Also, this webinar will be recorded, so

01:06.920 --> 01:12.440
don't worry if you have to hop early or if you want to revisit it in the future. All the

01:12.440 --> 01:18.520
registrants will get access to the recording, so you can always come back to it. Now, without further

01:18.600 --> 01:25.880
ado, let's welcome our panelists to the stage. So welcome, Ben, Kavya, and Jared. First, let me

01:25.880 --> 01:31.800
do a quick introduction of myself. So I am Will Chase. I am a senior product designer here at

01:31.800 --> 01:38.920
Observable. But prior to joining Observable, I did work in data journalism for about four years.

01:39.880 --> 01:47.320
I used to run the visual storytelling team at Axios, so returning to my roots here.

01:48.680 --> 01:52.280
So first, I want to welcome Ben Welsh to the stage.

01:53.880 --> 02:01.800
Hello. Welcome, Ben. Hello, hello. So Ben is a reporter, editor, and computer programmer. He

02:01.800 --> 02:07.800
is the founder of the Reuters News Application Desk, which covers the world's most pressing

02:07.800 --> 02:13.960
stories by developing dashboards, databases, and other automated systems. Welcome, Ben.

02:14.760 --> 02:19.720
Thanks for having me. Great. Next, I'm going to welcome Jared Whalen.

02:21.960 --> 02:27.080
Hello, Jared. Okay. So Jared is a Philadelphia-based journalist engineer

02:27.720 --> 02:33.960
at Polygraph and The Pudding. Previously, he's worked at Axios, the Philadelphia Inquirer,

02:33.960 --> 02:39.000
and the Delaware News Journal. His background includes visual storytelling,

02:39.000 --> 02:41.800
web application development, and data journalism.

02:44.280 --> 02:49.640
All right. And finally, let me welcome Kavya Beharaj to the stage.

02:50.440 --> 02:56.440
Hey. Hello. Welcome, Kavya. So Kavya is a designer and developer based in Brooklyn.

02:57.160 --> 03:02.280
She is currently an associate editor of data visualization at Axios. And before that,

03:02.280 --> 03:08.440
she worked in the nonprofit sector managing data projects, building web apps, and training others

03:08.440 --> 03:16.280
to better understand and use their data. All right. So thank you so much, everybody,

03:16.280 --> 03:25.160
for being here. We can go ahead and jump right into our discussion questions. So first, we are

03:25.160 --> 03:30.680
going to do a little bit of show and tell. So I'm going to ask each of our participants to share

03:30.680 --> 03:36.280
what they felt was one of the most impactful visualizations that they have made or worked on.

03:37.160 --> 03:42.360
And talk a little bit about it. Talk about, you know, what they think made it so impactful.

03:43.880 --> 03:47.080
So let's go ahead and start with Ben. Go for it, Ben.

03:48.040 --> 03:54.520
Hey. So I'm going to share my screen, and we're going to step into a time machine

03:55.160 --> 04:02.440
back to the year 2012. Who remembers that? And this is a graphic I worked on at the time with

04:02.440 --> 04:06.520
Robert Lopez and Kate Lithicum, two of my colleagues then at the Los Angeles Times

04:07.240 --> 04:14.120
in Southern California. And we put together this map, which is called How Fast is LAFD,

04:14.120 --> 04:20.360
where you live, to chart the response times to 911 calls by the local fire department

04:20.360 --> 04:25.720
there in the city. And you see it visualized there on the map, that sort of sideways key

04:25.720 --> 04:30.920
shape, believe it or not, is the actual city limits of Los Angeles itself. And it's surrounded

04:30.920 --> 04:36.280
by 87 other suburbs in LA County. And so if you ever see a weird shape like that, that's

04:36.280 --> 04:42.600
just because that's how the city was drawn up more than 100 years ago. And that is the

04:42.600 --> 04:47.320
jurisdiction of the city fire department that respond not just, of course, to structure fires

04:48.440 --> 04:54.200
and wildfires within the city limits, but primarily to emergency medical care situations.

04:54.920 --> 05:02.040
And there had emerged at the time, a sort of political controversy on a then very popular

05:02.040 --> 05:08.520
local news website called the Huffington Post, where a candidate for mayor who is trying to

05:08.520 --> 05:15.720
run for mayor again now in 2026, believe it or not, made some allegations about the fire department

05:15.720 --> 05:21.000
being substandard and responding to 911 calls. And that created a little bit of a political

05:21.000 --> 05:26.360
controversy and kind of raised the question within the politics of the city about what's

05:26.360 --> 05:32.040
going on at the fire department. And then curiously, the fire department invited me and

05:32.040 --> 05:36.440
Kate Lint to come over one day because we were asking, what's up? You got any data? Let's try to

05:36.440 --> 05:43.560
answer the questions from this Politico. And they called us over to City Hall East, if you've been

05:43.560 --> 05:47.480
there in downtown LA and put us in a conference room and they said, well, we got to tell you guys

05:47.480 --> 05:52.680
something. All the statistics we've been publishing for the last five or two years or so,

05:52.680 --> 05:58.520
they're all wrong. You know, the guy who had been our stats guy, well, he retired and it just kind

05:58.520 --> 06:02.360
of been on autopilot and we were looking at him and we realized he just did the math wrong.

06:03.640 --> 06:08.360
It was one of the stranger sort of moments as a reporter to just like be invited in for a

06:08.360 --> 06:13.800
confession, you know, and I think it really was kind of a well-meaning moment. They were trying

06:13.800 --> 06:17.720
to be transparent, but it's also pretty embarrassing, right? And so the front page

06:17.720 --> 06:22.360
of the newspaper the next day had a story by Kate and I that basically said, look, they say

06:22.360 --> 06:27.960
they don't know, right? And that just made the question an even bigger question. What the heck

06:27.960 --> 06:34.280
is going on with 911 at the city? This candidate had made very specific allegations. He couldn't

06:34.280 --> 06:40.200
really back him up and we couldn't, the department didn't really have an answer. So we filed a public

06:40.200 --> 06:46.040
records request for the complete database of millions and millions of 911 calls to try to

06:46.040 --> 06:50.040
answer the question ourselves, which we felt was kind of now in the public interest. And there's

06:50.040 --> 06:54.120
a whole long story I won't bore you with about fighting for the FOIA and getting the data and all

06:54.120 --> 06:58.920
the characters and blah, blah, blah. But at the end of the day, we kind of did our own analysis of

06:58.920 --> 07:04.520
those millions of calls and we used an outside standard, which is always a really powerful way

07:04.520 --> 07:10.760
to structure any kind of accountability work you do with data. So you see color coding on this map

07:10.760 --> 07:16.760
where green is good and purple is bad, pretty standard. But those thresholds and those cutoffs

07:16.760 --> 07:21.480
and the colors were all based on sort of the national response time standards of how fast

07:21.480 --> 07:26.520
calls are supposed to be. And that was a standard that the fire department itself had embraced and

07:26.520 --> 07:31.800
said it was doing well on. And so we were able to bring to bear sort of an outside standard that

07:31.800 --> 07:36.120
allowed us to evaluate, you know, kind of transparently and clearly and with, you know,

07:36.120 --> 07:41.320
colors on a map accountability or bring accountability to the question. And, you know,

07:41.320 --> 07:46.120
in any data visualization, if you want to get more investigative, you know, having that outside

07:46.120 --> 07:51.640
standard or something to measure up against that isn't just like according to you or according to

07:51.640 --> 07:56.680
its standard deviation in the distribution, but some real benchmark, right? So we had that strong

07:56.680 --> 08:02.280
benchmark and then it turned out, believe it or not, we had a reveal, you know, and that's a bit

08:02.280 --> 08:07.320
of Reuters ease. That's the term we use here at Reuters. When your report is going to tell the

08:07.320 --> 08:12.520
world something, it doesn't already know. And to me, that's the second piece that makes any powerful

08:12.520 --> 08:18.760
investigation really break through. And, you know, the reveal really was that the politicians claims

08:18.760 --> 08:23.160
were all wrong. You know what I mean? But that really was when you looked at the data closely,

08:23.480 --> 08:28.360
there were two or three really significant weaknesses and how 911 care is being delivered

08:28.360 --> 08:33.000
that were not being discussed in the city like at all. Like we're not even on the agenda. No one

08:33.000 --> 08:37.960
even talked about them. And they really surfaced and came up out of the data. And then we use that

08:37.960 --> 08:44.280
data to follow it out into the world and report on real world cases and show the impact and stakes.

08:44.280 --> 08:50.040
And so this visualization ran online as an interactive map, allowing people to personalize

08:50.040 --> 08:55.000
the story, which, of course, is another classic way to bring data out in journalism to look up

08:55.000 --> 08:59.960
their neighborhoods, but also to kind of tell the overall story, which ran on the front page of the

08:59.960 --> 09:04.840
print edition the same day. There's the print map that my colleague Tom Lauder put together

09:04.840 --> 09:09.800
with slightly different styles, but kind of the same idea. And you can see that the first

09:09.800 --> 09:13.640
angle we ran with the first day, and you might have to know LA to pull this out of the map,

09:14.280 --> 09:20.200
was that it was actually the richest neighborhoods in the city had the slowest response times,

09:20.200 --> 09:25.560
which is the Hollywood Hills where all the fancy movie stars live up at their mansions next to the

09:25.560 --> 09:31.640
Hollywood side. And the wealthier areas had the slowest response because they're in rural hillside

09:31.640 --> 09:36.440
communities is like the fundamental reason. But that was kind of the opposite of the way people

09:36.440 --> 09:42.200
had been talking about the issue. And so that was kind of a way into it that was kind of a reveal

09:42.200 --> 09:46.360
that gave you, hey, the truth of it is that it's actually the rich people who get the slowest

09:46.360 --> 09:55.080
response on average, and we built the whole story around that and told some real life cases of where

09:55.080 --> 10:00.440
people had died when calls had gone wrong in those areas. But if you look at the map closely,

10:00.440 --> 10:03.640
you'll see some other interesting things too, which is like, and this really stuck out to me

10:03.640 --> 10:08.840
at the time, is like, look, it's like the edge of the city on the borders is where it's really slow.

10:08.840 --> 10:13.880
What's up with that? No one talks about that. Well, we looked into it and it's common sense

10:13.880 --> 10:17.240
when you think about it, the fire trucks all sit at the fire stations. And if you're going to build

10:17.240 --> 10:23.160
108 fire stations, you don't put them next to the city border because you want to put them where

10:23.160 --> 10:28.680
they have a radius around them that they're going to serve. But then geospatially, you guys are all

10:28.680 --> 10:33.160
smart and dated people here, you have a weakness. You're then slower to get anywhere near the

10:33.160 --> 10:38.440
borders and particularly that long thin strip of the city that runs down to the harbor. And so we

10:38.440 --> 10:42.280
did a second story that was about how these boundaries and the lack of connections with

10:42.280 --> 10:48.680
neighboring fire departments to pool services was another big issue with the system. And then we did,

10:49.800 --> 10:54.440
this is a boring story I wrote because we needed to have one that day. But then we did a fourth

10:54.440 --> 10:59.880
one that was about how actually when you looked at the segments of the call, from when they pick

10:59.880 --> 11:03.960
up the phone to when someone arrives at your door, there's two or three segments in there of like

11:04.920 --> 11:10.600
triaging what happened, sending somebody getting to the place. We found that that early call taking

11:10.600 --> 11:14.600
period was actually where the biggest lag was in the whole system. And we were able to focus on

11:14.600 --> 11:19.320
that discrete flaw. And we did a whole lot of other coverage and lots and lots happened. And

11:19.320 --> 11:24.040
it was all tied up in city politics and an election that happened. But the result is,

11:24.040 --> 11:29.400
is the next mayor made really deep and significant changes to how the 9-1-1 system works,

11:29.400 --> 11:34.920
including revising how the call taking is done in the calling scripts. And also established a

11:34.920 --> 11:39.640
new statistical unit in the fire department to do the type of analysis we were doing internally

11:39.640 --> 11:46.040
within the department for itself. They called it fire stat. And the fire chief was replaced by,

11:47.640 --> 11:52.680
as well as part of the new administration. And then that ultimately kind of sent me down this

11:52.680 --> 11:57.640
road of becoming a fire department reporter, though never really planning to, which yielded

11:58.440 --> 12:03.240
later investigations about a failed building inspection program that led to overhauls there.

12:03.880 --> 12:12.040
And we were able to uncover and expose nepotism and abuse of the hiring process that led to

12:12.040 --> 12:17.480
complete reform of that as well and a few other things. And it all started with trying to make

12:17.480 --> 12:23.960
this map on my laptop in like summer of 2012. And so that's my long and boring tale. I hope it

12:23.960 --> 12:30.200
wasn't too long. No, not at all. I was going to say not boring in any way. I think it's actually,

12:31.400 --> 12:36.760
that's a really great story. Every step of the process, I was going to say,

12:37.400 --> 12:43.000
oh, and so which areas are the green areas that you noticed? Is it just because it's

12:43.000 --> 12:47.320
harder to get to? And then what was the result? Did they overhaul the things? So yeah,

12:47.320 --> 12:52.120
you answered all the questions. Yeah, the green areas are where the fire stations are.

12:52.920 --> 12:58.520
And so many other fire services will preposition units in high volume call

12:58.520 --> 13:03.000
zones rather than have them sit at the station. That's like one common response to that issue.

13:03.000 --> 13:07.080
The fire department has not done that in Los Angeles. And if you paid close attention to

13:07.080 --> 13:13.080
the Palisades fire earlier this year, one of the key criticisms of how that was managed is they

13:13.080 --> 13:18.520
did not preposition units in areas that were prone to fire, even though there was a very high risk

13:18.520 --> 13:23.000
fire warning at the time. My colleagues who are still at the LA Times did some great reporting

13:23.000 --> 13:27.880
showing how that structural flaw still exists. Yeah, because it does really look like it's

13:27.880 --> 13:35.000
essentially gridded across the city like that. Yeah. The other thing I think is amazing,

13:35.000 --> 13:41.640
I'll just call out is that you mentioned this was made in 2012, and it is still running on your

13:41.640 --> 13:49.320
browser here more than more than 10 years later. So it's always nice to see the longevity of things

13:49.320 --> 13:53.640
like that. I'll publish static files if you can, guys. That's my static files. There you go.

13:55.320 --> 13:59.640
All right. Jared, why don't you go? Why don't you go next?

14:02.680 --> 14:07.880
Okay, certainly. Hi, again. Hi, everybody. My name is Jared from Philadelphia. The project I

14:07.960 --> 14:14.120
want to show today is one that over at Polygraph, which is the internal studio of The Pudding,

14:14.120 --> 14:18.920
which I hope many of you are familiar with, a project that we recently published that I thought

14:19.800 --> 14:26.760
kind of covered a lot of different elements of being a data journalist. For this project,

14:26.760 --> 14:34.760
we partnered up with the Trans-Journalist Alliance Association to take a look at the question of

14:34.760 --> 14:41.400
how can we kind of audit the news industry and look at how they are exploring a particular topic,

14:41.400 --> 14:49.000
in this case, trans issues. The way we went about doing that is we put together a script that is

14:49.000 --> 14:56.840
using the Media Cloud API, which is an organization that basically compiles news articles by

14:57.400 --> 15:05.080
keyword search and just allows you to, while not actually pull the body text for obvious reasons,

15:05.080 --> 15:09.400
allow you to say, I want to see all the articles that include these keywords. I want to pull all

15:09.400 --> 15:15.080
the articles from the New York Times that mention this topic. From there, we just put together a

15:15.080 --> 15:21.080
long list of keywords, a long list of things that we felt captured the breadth of trans issues

15:21.080 --> 15:28.840
across healthcare, across pop culture, across social issues, and then we're able to generate

15:28.840 --> 15:35.880
this corpus of hundreds of thousands of articles over the course of the last five years. From there,

15:35.880 --> 15:43.320
we were then able to run the data through a large language model classifier to then take those

15:43.320 --> 15:49.320
articles and group them by themes that we predetermined. For example, we were able to

15:49.320 --> 15:55.240
take all those articles, filter out the noise, and then come out with 18,000 articles about

15:56.440 --> 16:01.960
healthcare and body autonomy. From there, we then were able to then go into each of these

16:01.960 --> 16:08.680
broader themes and cluster those articles by events. From here, we're not only looking at

16:08.680 --> 16:15.640
the 18,000 articles, but we were able to find those 150 events that group those articles together.

16:16.120 --> 16:20.680
And why do all this? Well, the reason we wanted to do this is because we wanted a way

16:20.680 --> 16:25.800
to hold ourselves in the news industry accountable for the stuff that we're covering. How are we

16:25.800 --> 16:32.920
talking about these issues? So in this case, when you look for this broader theme, we can see some

16:32.920 --> 16:38.520
of the major stories that pop out, such as convergent therapy going before the Supreme Court

16:38.520 --> 16:44.680
or puberty blocker bans. And what we hope to do is create an interactive experience that allows

16:44.760 --> 16:50.200
the user to really dive in, look at these individual articles, explore how different

16:50.200 --> 16:55.640
publications based off maybe how their political lean are, and see how they are covering it.

16:56.280 --> 17:02.040
And then what I really enjoy is because we partnered with this organization, we were able

17:02.040 --> 17:06.280
to bring in their insights. So we were able to have them come in and say like, well, here is how

17:06.280 --> 17:10.760
a topic is being covered. And here are some insights, how we think maybe this topic should

17:10.760 --> 17:15.720
be covered, or how us in the trans community believe that it should be covered. From there,

17:15.720 --> 17:21.000
we basically present the data in ways that we hopefully communicate the story, such as

17:21.720 --> 17:27.960
publication, political lean by year, giving the articles themselves, showing these little

17:27.960 --> 17:34.840
bee swarms for each topic of how, based on your current filtering, how the articles are published,

17:34.840 --> 17:43.800
which allows you to see jumps across time when a story is in the news. And what we want to hopefully

17:43.800 --> 17:49.240
do with all this is as this project continues to grow, because we have this running on an ongoing

17:49.240 --> 17:55.640
basis, is be able to take a look back at any given point and look for those stories, look for

17:55.880 --> 18:03.560
how, as the political climate changes and things in both the news, but as a way of us in the news

18:03.560 --> 18:09.240
industry, change how we think about toppings and how to cover things, be able to actually put some

18:09.240 --> 18:16.120
data behind some of those preconceived notions. The response to this project has been

18:16.120 --> 18:22.600
overwhelmingly successful, which we always want to hear. And my hope is that as we continue to

18:23.480 --> 18:30.280
maintain this project over the coming years, that we're able to see the news industry in general do

18:30.280 --> 18:36.200
a bit more inward looking, but taking, investing the time to do audits of your own reporting,

18:36.200 --> 18:40.760
your own coverage to see how you are reporting on the communities you read you.

18:43.880 --> 18:50.600
Wow, that's amazing. Thanks, Jared. So when you say it's updated, do you mean that this is a

18:51.560 --> 18:58.280
sort of semi-manual process that you guys are returning to, or it's more of an automated

18:58.840 --> 19:05.880
updating process? Yeah, great question. So the way it works now, we have a monthly script that will

19:05.880 --> 19:12.200
one, pull the entire corpus of articles and then run it through our model to handle the classification.

19:12.200 --> 19:17.480
But as anybody who's worked with large language models, who's worked with machine learning,

19:17.480 --> 19:22.840
you know that when you're working with machine learning, you need to constantly training your

19:22.840 --> 19:28.360
model. So the way that we have this is we have a handful of configuration files that as new data

19:28.360 --> 19:33.720
comes in, it gives us the ability to manually audit, to manually make corrections, and then

19:33.720 --> 19:40.200
continue to train the model going forward. So big picture of the way this works is on a monthly

19:40.200 --> 19:46.520
basis, it's doing classifications based off the existing data. And then we have a more semi-annual

19:46.520 --> 19:51.720
approach to kind of look at the entire corpus of data for reclassification purposes.

19:53.240 --> 19:58.360
Awesome. All right, let's go ahead and move on to Kavya.

20:08.360 --> 20:16.200
Kavya, I think you're muted actually. My bad. Oh, good. Okay, so this is a

20:16.280 --> 20:22.920
project that I worked on with Will and Jared actually, and a couple other folks on our team.

20:23.880 --> 20:29.320
And this was right after that summer with intense wildfire smoke on the east coast.

20:30.360 --> 20:37.000
So I in New York was looking outside my window and seeing just orange. So what we wanted to

20:37.000 --> 20:46.200
accomplish with this article is basically kind of recap what happened that summer, what made

20:46.200 --> 20:54.680
the wildfire smoke that summer so unusual, and what kind of it means if this is our new normal

20:54.680 --> 21:00.360
with pollution exposure. So this was a collaboration with a lot of different people.

21:01.000 --> 21:09.320
You know, but the part that I want to zoom in on is the zoom in section that focuses on

21:10.440 --> 21:17.480
kind of like Ben was saying, localizing the story to you. So this is basically a dashboard that

21:17.480 --> 21:24.360
lets you search for some metro areas and look up how your pollution exposure might have been

21:24.920 --> 21:31.720
in that time period compared to the average over the last, you know, 10 or so years.

21:33.080 --> 21:42.520
And this is a part of the motivation for doing this is because Axios has many local bureaus.

21:42.520 --> 21:50.200
So we have newsletters that operate in more than 30 US cities. And so we wanted a way to

21:50.200 --> 21:56.280
take this really large national story and make it relevant in some way to folks. So this dashboard

21:56.280 --> 22:03.320
and the data that I collected as part of it was for that effort. And also, you know,

22:04.360 --> 22:09.960
including some tips for how to actually protect yourself from wildfire smoke because that's

22:09.960 --> 22:18.680
something we need to do now. So part of the design decisions that went into this

22:19.640 --> 22:26.280
was kind of trying to make a chart that can kind of speak on its own terms.

22:26.840 --> 22:33.240
So if you just look at it, it tells a complete story. This is the most recent data. This is

22:33.240 --> 22:39.240
where this is how it compares to the average, and a pretty clear indication of whether what

22:39.240 --> 22:46.120
you're experiencing is healthy or unhealthy, according to the standard set by the government.

22:46.840 --> 22:53.400
So one of the effects or the impacts of like taking the time to scrape this data and clean

22:53.400 --> 23:00.280
it and prepare it is that our local markets were able to run stories based on it. So it wasn't just

23:00.280 --> 23:07.960
published once. The stories got used a lot after that too, because we took the time to localize it.

23:08.520 --> 23:14.600
So yeah, that's all. Also, the folks who built this are also on the call. So if you want to

23:14.600 --> 23:23.960
say anything about it as well, you both are welcome. I can do a quick answer to this question

23:23.960 --> 23:28.120
that Amanda just asked in the chat. How did you create the smoke animations on scroll?

23:29.480 --> 23:32.600
So yeah, that was another collaboration. A lot of people worked on this section.

23:34.440 --> 23:42.280
Essentially, this is a background image, which is the map. So it's a static image. So that map was

23:42.360 --> 23:54.280
created in a combination of tools, QGIS and Adobe Illustrator. And then we pulled the data for

23:56.520 --> 24:05.560
how much smoke there is. So NOAA publishes satellite data that you can process to

24:05.560 --> 24:14.360
get this density of smoke. And then we processed those images to create transparent versions that

24:14.360 --> 24:18.280
just have the brown portion where it's the intensity of smoke and the rest of the image

24:18.280 --> 24:23.000
is transparent. And so then those are overlaid on top of that background map image.

24:23.720 --> 24:26.360
And Jared worked on a

24:28.920 --> 24:36.920
scrolly telling a little bit of code that would cycle those images. So it steps through all the

24:36.920 --> 24:44.440
smoke images. We have like one image, I think, for every four hours, I want to say. I don't

24:44.440 --> 24:50.760
remember if that's exactly right. But it steps through, you know, all the hundreds of images

24:50.760 --> 24:55.640
for the smoke that we have that represent that time period. Yeah.

24:59.320 --> 25:06.040
All right. Thank you, everybody. So moving on now into a couple more questions just about how

25:06.040 --> 25:12.760
things work in newsrooms, how you deal with data. And anybody, feel free to jump in here.

25:13.400 --> 25:18.200
So when you're handling a messy data set, what is your first step in figuring out

25:18.200 --> 25:29.000
if there's a story worth telling? I can start. I think that a good first step, or at least

25:29.000 --> 25:33.160
what should be a good first step, is when you get a messy data set, or really any data set,

25:33.160 --> 25:38.040
but especially one that's messy and a bit complicated, is before you jump in to try

25:38.040 --> 25:44.280
to tell the story with it, before you jump in to try to find a narrative, is you make sure you

25:44.280 --> 25:49.640
figure out why this data set even exists and how it's already being used. It's very tempting to

25:49.640 --> 25:54.920
dive right into it and inject your interpretation of what it could tell you, especially if it is

25:54.920 --> 26:00.920
messy and maybe it presents some like story agnostic challenges like pulling from a PDF or doing some

26:00.920 --> 26:07.320
weird scraping, because a lot of us in this field see that and while it may be tedious, we actually

26:07.320 --> 26:12.440
kind of get excited about figuring out that problem. But what can often end up happening

26:12.440 --> 26:17.240
is you spend a lot of time to get the data to where you think it should look like, only to find

26:17.240 --> 26:22.680
it doesn't actually tell you what you think. So whether it's referring to documentation, whether

26:22.680 --> 26:28.360
it's seeing how other news outlets or other places are using that data, or hopping on the phone and

26:28.360 --> 26:33.160
talking with the data manager, those are pretty much what should be your first step anytime you

26:33.160 --> 26:40.760
encounter a new data set. Yeah, and I would say another technique is to just really focus on the

26:40.760 --> 26:47.160
fundamentals of the table itself. What is a row? And I mean that in the most philosophical

26:47.160 --> 26:50.920
and Socratic sense, you know what I mean? Like said, what does each row represent? What is it

26:50.920 --> 26:56.520
recording, right? What does each column indicate? Like I had an interesting example with some

26:56.520 --> 27:01.160
students I was working with a couple years ago at DePaul University, where they had been given a data

27:01.160 --> 27:07.400
set from the city of Chicago that was trees planted by the city tree planting program. We were going

27:07.400 --> 27:11.080
to look, you know, they had set a target for how many they were going to grow. There's your benchmark

27:11.080 --> 27:16.120
guys. And we were going to map it to see who got the trees. It's almost like I do this same story

27:16.120 --> 27:22.920
over and over again. And we did our provisional analysis, and went back to the city to kind of

27:22.920 --> 27:26.760
tell them what we had found. And we learned something a little embarrassing, which was that

27:26.760 --> 27:32.840
there was a column with a very cryptic name that we didn't know what it was, and didn't think too

27:32.840 --> 27:37.800
much about. But it was actually the number of trees that had been planted in that row. So each

27:37.800 --> 27:42.760
row was not a tree, which is what we were working under the assumption of. Each row was a work order

27:42.760 --> 27:49.800
to plant one too many trees, right? And so our initial analysis was kind of fundamentally wrong,

27:49.800 --> 27:56.120
because we didn't understand, you know, everything we needed to about the data. What is a row,

27:56.120 --> 28:01.640
and what does each column mean? And just slowing down and forcing yourself to walk through that

28:01.640 --> 28:10.120
is, I think, crucial. Yeah, I think my thoughts on that were pretty much covered. But I think

28:10.840 --> 28:17.720
there is also a way that focusing on one row of data can also make clear what steps are needed

28:17.720 --> 28:24.200
to clean it. You know, as you're trying to understand what is actually being shown, maybe

28:24.200 --> 28:29.720
it's multiple things at once. It's not just one observation, you know, maybe there's multiple

28:29.720 --> 28:34.840
data sets mixed together, they all need to be teased apart. And that's another thing that can

28:34.840 --> 28:39.960
come out of just trying to understand the data on a basic level. And you can trust your gut,

28:39.960 --> 28:44.280
if something is confusing to you, then it's probably going to be confusing to other people.

28:46.280 --> 28:51.800
Yeah, that's great. Jared, I love your point about like, just pick up the phone and call somebody,

28:51.800 --> 28:57.880
because, you know, I think so many of us in the data world or who come from a background that

28:57.880 --> 29:04.120
might be more like engineering focused. That's just like never where your mind would go. At first,

29:04.120 --> 29:08.920
right? Your mind goes to like, Oh, what code can I write to do this? Or how can I investigate this?

29:08.920 --> 29:14.200
And I remember there's a hilarious quote, Hadley Wickham, who, you know, works at our studio,

29:14.200 --> 29:19.800
he went to a journalism conference one time. And he said that after talking to people, he said,

29:20.760 --> 29:25.480
the thing I learned the most is that like, what you can learn by just picking up the phone

29:25.480 --> 29:31.400
is incredible. And that is a thing that I would never, literally never do in my life.

29:32.680 --> 29:36.200
So yes, pick up the phone, don't be afraid of it.

29:38.680 --> 29:42.360
Kavya, I actually wanted to come back to you for the next question. I was,

29:43.960 --> 29:48.600
because I know that you've had some experience working in other industries, you mentioned you've

29:48.600 --> 29:55.560
worked in nonprofits, and done some, you know, sort of dashboarding work in in the business space.

29:56.440 --> 30:02.200
So I know a lot of people in our audience create visualizations for businesses or internal teams,

30:02.200 --> 30:09.240
dashboards, that sort of thing. And what principles do from data journalism, do you

30:09.240 --> 30:14.760
think that they can apply to make their own work more impactful? Of course, anyone else is welcome

30:14.760 --> 30:22.440
to jump into it. But yeah, yeah, totally. I think in my past job, I've had a lot of experience

30:22.440 --> 30:29.560
making dashboards that I'm not sure anyone actually used or saw. And I think part of the

30:29.560 --> 30:37.000
challenge there is that we're going in assuming that we need a dashboard, you know, and that all

30:37.000 --> 30:42.040
of the data points that we want to visualize matter. But I think there are more fundamental

30:42.040 --> 30:47.560
questions, you know, like, why do we need to visualize all this in this way, who is going to

30:47.560 --> 30:55.320
be using it and how, and how is it going to be maintained, which is was my job before this.

30:56.760 --> 31:03.080
And so I think that those are principles from data journalism that apply anywhere.

31:03.080 --> 31:09.800
Like anytime you want to do some sort of visual communication or chart, it is really helpful to

31:09.800 --> 31:17.720
understand who you are trying to help, to influence, to inform. And making something

31:17.720 --> 31:25.720
that's actually useful starts with figuring out why it exists. That's my take.

31:26.920 --> 31:34.920
Yeah, that's great. I love that. All right, let me let me ask for the room a little bit about

31:35.160 --> 31:42.680
about collaboration. So a lot of the pieces we showed here in the in the sort of show and tell

31:42.680 --> 31:46.920
section, you know, there were many names on those or lots of people that were involved in those

31:46.920 --> 31:51.800
projects, you know, you've got reporters, designers, editors, developers, all kinds of people, artists

31:51.800 --> 31:59.800
working on them. So what have any of you learned about working across roles that might help other

31:59.800 --> 32:03.480
people building data visualizations in collaborative environments?

32:06.280 --> 32:12.360
I think something that I learned, especially working at Axios is just the importance of

32:12.920 --> 32:18.360
like that it's okay to let go. I know a lot of newsroom developers, but I imagine a lot of the

32:18.360 --> 32:22.920
people who work in smaller companies as well. You're probably one of the few technical people

32:22.920 --> 32:27.400
on your team, you might very well be the only designer, the only developer, the only data

32:27.400 --> 32:33.000
journalist in your newsroom in your organization. So you might get used to being the jack of all

32:33.000 --> 32:38.440
trades and feeling very good about knowing, yeah, I could do this whole thing by myself. But when

32:38.440 --> 32:42.920
you find yourself in a collaborative environment, you often are and probably shouldn't be the best

32:42.920 --> 32:49.560
designer, the best developer, the best data journalist, all these things at once. So just

32:49.560 --> 32:54.760
learning to be comfortable and then kind of put your ego aside. Let people who shine at something

32:54.760 --> 32:59.000
let them shine at it. And not only will that make a better, a smoother and probably better

32:59.560 --> 33:03.560
process and probably better product in the end, it also will allow you to grow and achieve your

33:03.560 --> 33:18.040
preferred specialty. Something I might add to that is also from experience working here at Axios

33:18.040 --> 33:27.640
is that it helps to let the story steer the ship. As in every piece, every person who's

33:27.640 --> 33:34.120
contributing to the story is supporting the whole. And so the visual has to work with the words and

33:34.120 --> 33:40.120
has to work with the style and has to work with the social strategy. And all of those pieces are

33:40.120 --> 33:47.960
ultimately like our mission is to produce the best story possible and to tell a specific

33:47.960 --> 33:56.200
narrative. And it is humbling and also helpful to have that to fall back on when we're all trying

33:56.200 --> 34:13.640
to do our own little pieces of a story. Great. All right. Well, I think maybe the

34:13.640 --> 34:22.680
final question we can talk about before we go on to some Q&A section is I'd love if anybody wants

34:22.680 --> 34:31.080
to talk about how either technology or tooling for making visualizations has changed over the

34:31.080 --> 34:38.280
course of your careers and some sort of how has that impacted your work or maybe even what are

34:38.280 --> 34:42.520
you excited about for the future? Anything on the horizon that you're interested in in that space?

34:42.520 --> 34:55.400
I'm getting old. That's kind of scary to reflect on. I mean, I would say there's a few things. I

34:55.400 --> 35:01.160
think it's easy to take the cloud computing revolution for granted, just the fact that

35:01.160 --> 35:09.880
we're all able to just publish stuff from our MacBook with whatever flavor of platform you prefer.

35:09.880 --> 35:15.000
That wasn't the case when I started my career. You'd show up and they'd be like, you need an edge

35:15.000 --> 35:22.440
server in the basement to do anything and that costs X thousand dollars or whatever. That really

35:22.440 --> 35:33.560
I think opened up more than we appreciate, I think, in terms of opportunity. And then I just have to

35:33.560 --> 35:41.720
say, I think open source software just continues to be the seedbed and fuel or whatever the right

35:41.720 --> 35:46.520
metaphor is for all of it. I think sadly we've seen a decline of that in recent years in the

35:46.520 --> 35:50.840
news industry, which I presented a report on recently with my colleague Scott Klein.

35:52.440 --> 35:57.720
And I'm sorry to see that, but I'm proud to be here with Observable, who I know is one of the

35:57.720 --> 36:02.040
great leaders in that. So we just all need to contribute and keep investing in it because it

36:02.040 --> 36:11.080
does pay off. I think one thing that has definitely changed, I've been doing this for

36:11.080 --> 36:16.280
about a decade now, and the barrier to entry on getting to just getting started with a project

36:16.280 --> 36:21.160
is certainly lower. A lot of that comes from the open source stuff that was just mentioned. A lot

36:21.160 --> 36:27.720
of that comes from just more tools like DataRapper, like Flourish, like Observable, like all these ways

36:27.720 --> 36:36.440
that you can dive into DataViz or data storytelling or coding in general. But also just the amount

36:36.440 --> 36:42.360
of information there's. I mean, a lot of us I'm sure have had the problem of scouring the internet

36:42.360 --> 36:48.600
for some obscure Stack Overflow reference that had to fix some bug. And whether you're using

36:48.600 --> 36:52.520
something like whether using AI, whether you're using just Google getting better at searching,

36:52.520 --> 36:57.400
whether there's just more stuff out there, or maybe you're using like a Slack community.

36:57.400 --> 37:02.280
I think that it's never been a better time to start learning this stuff because you can get

37:02.280 --> 37:08.920
started so much faster. You don't need to struggle alone. There really is a very large community

37:08.920 --> 37:17.320
and ecosystem of information out there. Yeah, I think related to that is the amount of tools

37:17.320 --> 37:24.280
that we can keep on our tool belt is a lot bigger now because they have been so more established

37:24.280 --> 37:28.840
and there's a lower barrier to entry. And I think that's also exciting in the way that we can get

37:28.840 --> 37:37.640
more experimental with our data storytelling and make things that we imagine more real quicker.

37:41.080 --> 37:47.560
Yeah, totally. Great answers. All right. Time to move on to Q&A section. So we've got lots of great

37:47.560 --> 37:51.960
questions. I think a couple of these questions may have already been answered in the chat. So I'm

37:51.960 --> 38:04.680
going to jump around a little bit. So let me start with a question from Pedro. So Pedro asks

38:05.800 --> 38:11.000
about impacting the reader. In visualization, we generally encode information through visual

38:11.000 --> 38:17.480
variables such as symbols, shapes, sizes, colors, et cetera. This turns data about people into

38:17.480 --> 38:23.640
visual abstractions. How do you think we can humanize visualization in humanitarian content

38:23.640 --> 38:28.280
themes such as war, urban violence, discrimination, poverty, et cetera?

38:34.520 --> 38:40.760
I mean, I can just start. I think this is a huge and constant challenge that we face.

38:40.760 --> 38:48.680
I remember working on a project with some of their Axios folks on when we hit the million

38:48.680 --> 38:54.120
COVID deaths milestone. And I feel like that's also when this challenge came to the forefront

38:54.120 --> 39:01.160
for a lot of publications. How do we visualize, how do we get people to appreciate the size of

39:01.160 --> 39:11.000
one million deaths? And that might be a good example to look back on as different newsrooms,

39:11.000 --> 39:22.600
each one took their own approach. But I think it has to come from a place of empathy and trying to

39:22.600 --> 39:30.920
give people comparisons and ways in so they can relate to what might be colder numbers

39:30.920 --> 39:37.880
into something that they can understand and relate to. And then, you know, the impact happens.

39:40.920 --> 39:48.840
Yeah. I was just going to say that I think this is a question that's very important and it's hard

39:48.840 --> 39:54.760
because at the end of the day, as communicators, sometimes the most clear way to communicate

39:54.760 --> 40:00.680
something can come across as cold. And I think that can also, I think we think about this in terms

40:00.680 --> 40:05.960
of like databases, but the same thing can definitely be true in the traditional written

40:05.960 --> 40:13.480
journal or in video. And like, I think we all know examples of news content, whether it's a video,

40:13.480 --> 40:19.560
whether it's an article that just rubs you the wrong way or clearly doesn't have empathy or is

40:19.560 --> 40:26.520
clearly talking about the subject in a way that just misses the mark. And a lot of times it's less

40:26.520 --> 40:32.760
to do with what symbols are used or what colors are used and just how the general feel of it.

40:32.760 --> 40:40.280
So getting your content in front of people and sharing it with them. If you have a diverse group

40:40.280 --> 40:44.520
of voices in your newsroom, have them look at it, have them look at your content and see this

40:44.520 --> 40:51.480
anything strike you in a way that maybe we've missed. And I think that's probably a better

40:51.480 --> 40:57.720
approach than just saying, oh, here's our color palette that we think is good for this topic. Well,

40:57.720 --> 41:05.000
in all circumstances, is that going to be enough? Yeah, and this is a really great question and a

41:05.000 --> 41:09.480
big challenge. I know it's one that my colleagues in our excellent Reuters graphics department take

41:09.480 --> 41:15.160
super seriously. You know, I just posted in the chat a link to their work overall, but to two,

41:15.160 --> 41:19.880
I think, kind of outstanding examples of dealing with this. One by my colleague, Mario Zafra,

41:19.880 --> 41:25.000
and another by Ali Levine. And, you know, in both cases, you know, in almost all cases,

41:25.000 --> 41:30.840
our Reuters team in graphics is trying to use illustration and other more creative

41:30.840 --> 41:38.440
approaches to design, to humanize and to connect. And I think just showing people in Mario's case

41:38.440 --> 41:43.880
is one good example. You know what I mean? Not letting it be dots, you know, is one classic

41:43.880 --> 41:50.760
approach that goes back to the Isotype, you know, and some of the foundational tools of data

41:50.760 --> 41:58.920
visualization. And it's not my forte, personally. And so every time they put something out, I'm

41:58.920 --> 42:05.000
always kind of impressed and jealous of how they're able to bring their creativity and their artistic

42:05.000 --> 42:10.280
skills to bear. And I think we should, you know, as we can do more and more with data, and I can

42:10.280 --> 42:16.760
say more and more about cloud computing and Amazon, you know, lambdas and AI, blah, blah, blah.

42:16.760 --> 42:21.560
You know, we can't, we need more of that in journalism, but we cannot lose that artistic

42:21.560 --> 42:31.320
side of graphics, which is just so important. And, you know, I will not be replicated by AI anytime

42:31.320 --> 42:39.480
soon. Yeah, very true. Well, that's a great segue, maybe, into the next question. I knew it was

42:39.480 --> 42:49.080
coming. Okay. Generative AI. So, Ernesto asks, besides direct help in faster slash better coding,

42:49.720 --> 42:54.280
how do you think that generative AI is transforming the way we do storytelling with data?

42:55.160 --> 42:57.240
And it would be great if you have any examples.

43:02.280 --> 43:07.080
I think it's going to increase, I mean, to use kind of a loaded term, I think it's going to

43:07.080 --> 43:11.160
increase the accessibility of the type of work we do. It's going to make, it's going to bring

43:11.160 --> 43:17.560
more people into the conversation and allow and bring down the barriers or entry to just like

43:17.560 --> 43:24.440
figuring out how to get something on the internet or turn the data into a chart. And I'm seeing that

43:24.440 --> 43:30.440
every day here in our Reuters newsroom, where we recently launched a internal chatbot that through

43:30.440 --> 43:35.480
an MCP server is able to make charts and data wrapper just by having a chat. And we're going

43:35.480 --> 43:40.600
to pull in access to our financial data terminal next. So, you could have a chat that says,

43:40.600 --> 43:46.680
hey, can you get me the Apple stock price over the last 12 months and do it as a percentage change

43:46.680 --> 43:51.080
versus the S&P and Michael to make a line chart. And just by doing those kind of chatting maneuvers,

43:51.080 --> 43:55.320
you're able to publish a chart. We actually did one that way and put it on the homepage just two

43:55.320 --> 44:00.680
days ago. It was the first example. That one was by me because it's my job to be a full-time nerd.

44:00.680 --> 44:07.800
But I just see so much evidence that those types of interfaces or whoever you were going to call

44:07.800 --> 44:12.040
them are just going to put data and graphics tools in the hands of so many more people.

44:12.600 --> 44:17.000
And there'll be bad things to come out of that, but I suspect there'll be a lot more good things.

44:20.200 --> 44:24.360
Yeah, I think like obviously there's so much that can be said about how the news

44:25.320 --> 44:33.160
can use AI. But thinking for me personally, I tend to approach it just as like another tool,

44:33.160 --> 44:38.760
and every tool has its limitations. Every tool is good at something and bad at others. And what I

44:38.760 --> 44:46.600
would hope is that you will see more data storytellers using it for the things that humans are

44:46.600 --> 44:52.280
bad at and maybe not letting it just replace the things that hopefully everybody in this call like

44:52.280 --> 44:56.440
the fun parts of the job, the interesting parts of the job, and just trying to find that balance

44:56.440 --> 45:03.080
for you as you tell stories. Can this improve your work? If so, that's great, but just be aware

45:03.080 --> 45:08.840
that it is a tool and tools require the user to know what they're doing. Yeah, I recently had a

45:08.840 --> 45:13.560
conversation with Marco Hernandez from the New York Times, a great graphics artist at the School

45:13.560 --> 45:20.360
of Visual Arts. And he said, let the AI do the monkey tasks is the way he put it. But you always

45:20.360 --> 45:24.840
have to remember that you are the protagonist, which I thought was quite a zinger from Marco.

45:27.880 --> 45:36.040
Yeah, I think one way we're experimenting with AI at Axios is one of our reporters set up a chat bot

45:36.040 --> 45:43.720
that we fed in kind of some standards around data visualization and data that we have. And

45:44.520 --> 45:50.440
it's basically to help reporters triage a data set when they encounter it in the wild to see,

45:50.440 --> 45:56.680
you know, is this suitable for visualization? Is there any like pitfalls that might, you know,

45:56.680 --> 46:03.800
like missing data or things to be, you know, to be aware of when considering whether to cover it?

46:04.360 --> 46:13.080
But it's far from being gritty for prime time. But it's an interesting idea. I think

46:16.760 --> 46:25.160
it's exposing kind of how nuanced our job is, because there aren't always set rules for,

46:25.160 --> 46:32.040
for, you know, evaluating data sets. But even getting a reporter like 80% of the way might be

46:32.040 --> 46:41.320
helpful too. So worth both sides of worth pursuing, I think. Totally. All right, I know we're going

46:41.320 --> 46:47.240
on a little long here. But I do just want to get to this final question that we can close out with,

46:47.240 --> 46:53.400
because I know this is a question I get a lot of questions about this personally. And so I know a

46:53.400 --> 46:59.960
lot of people are curious about behind the scenes, like kind of a little bit of the plumbing of of

46:59.960 --> 47:07.320
how these visual stories and data journalism comes together in the real world. So there's a

47:07.320 --> 47:11.480
lot of aspects to this question. I think we can all go around maybe and talk about one or two of

47:11.480 --> 47:18.440
them. So current asks, I'm curious about tooling and collaboration in newsrooms. Does it look like

47:18.440 --> 47:24.840
Figma designers and then devs implementing those mocks? Or is it more like designing in code?

47:25.480 --> 47:30.040
How do you share work in progress? Do you have an internal staging environment?

47:30.040 --> 47:34.920
How do you share multiple prototypes internally for feedback? How does it migrate to production?

47:39.160 --> 47:42.600
There's not going to be one answer. You know, newsrooms are kind of all over the place.

47:42.600 --> 47:48.200
Everybody is is solving the same problems in slightly different ways. And so we could probably,

47:48.200 --> 47:53.080
if we tried with some post-it notes, come up with like, here's the 25 tricks, and then everybody,

47:53.080 --> 47:59.080
every newsroom is some mix and match of those 25 would be my hunch. In my newsroom experience,

47:59.080 --> 48:05.160
which is now more than 20 years of doing this, I've yet to encounter a newsroom team that runs

48:05.160 --> 48:10.200
like a truly formal product team, you know what I mean with that type of division of labor. It's

48:10.200 --> 48:15.000
more and more common for a team like ours to partner with our product team. And then they

48:15.000 --> 48:20.440
kind of force us into their their agile process or, you know, whatever you might call it. I think

48:20.440 --> 48:25.640
there's a lot of merit to that. But I think it's pretty unusual to see that in the newsroom, which

48:25.640 --> 48:34.680
which tends to run in a little more of a chaotic fashion. In my experience, and just having done

48:34.680 --> 48:40.440
this about a lot of people, I think you're going to see a lot of website development that's story

48:40.440 --> 48:46.040
specific being done in a static site framework that is roughly like observable framework,

48:46.040 --> 48:53.480
to be honest with you, it's going to be some strange Frankenstein combination of node and

48:53.480 --> 48:59.560
templating tools, maybe involving svelter react, maybe still being on an old templating tag thing.

49:01.000 --> 49:06.840
And every newsroom has its own odd variation on that that is then used to bake out or, you know,

49:07.480 --> 49:15.000
build a static distribution. That's just such a sturdy approach to publishing custom

49:15.560 --> 49:20.440
micro sites or whatever you want to call them, that I think it's pretty much been irresistible,

49:20.440 --> 49:24.840
you know. But there are many other ways things happen. And I don't want to

49:24.840 --> 49:28.840
bore you by doing a laundry list. But that's my impression. What do you guys think?

49:30.840 --> 49:35.640
Yeah, I think that it's always going to depend on the project and the person building it,

49:35.640 --> 49:41.720
specifically looking at the design by code or do it in Figma. I think every single time,

49:42.680 --> 49:50.520
especially when it's like a data heavy story, the data very much will inform the shape of it.

49:50.520 --> 49:54.840
If you do the whole thing in Figma first, whenever I'm developing, I'm thinking, man,

49:54.840 --> 49:58.600
I really wish we knew more about the data before we made these Figma designs. And then the other

49:58.600 --> 50:02.920
way around, when I'm designing my code, it's like, wow, this would be a lot easier if we had

50:02.920 --> 50:08.680
some cleaner prototypes designed out. So I think it just really is going to be on a case by case

50:08.680 --> 50:14.760
basis. Because especially if you're not doing the same thing every time, and it is more bespoke,

50:14.760 --> 50:20.280
more bespoke visualizations, more bespoke data story experiences, there's always going to be

50:20.280 --> 50:25.000
nuances that you probably wouldn't find in traditional static site building.

50:27.400 --> 50:34.520
Yeah, I think our approach varies based on timeline and who's leading the project. I think

50:34.520 --> 50:41.640
everyone is familiar with a slightly different suite of tools. So what I might do in a spreadsheet,

50:41.640 --> 50:48.440
my colleague might do an R, but we end up in the same place. I think in terms of feedback,

50:49.400 --> 50:55.240
Axios is kind of unique nowadays in that we're fully remote and a pretty small newsroom

50:55.240 --> 51:02.200
comparatively. So everything we do is on Slack, for better or worse. And a lot of the processes

51:02.200 --> 51:10.520
we have are kind of cobbled together slash what we personally think is the best approach at the

51:10.520 --> 51:19.480
moment. But that's also playing to our strengths. It's so unique to us as a team. So I think that's

51:20.760 --> 51:23.400
yeah, just echoing what folks are saying, it's going to vary.

51:23.400 --> 51:33.880
Yeah, totally. All right. Well, thank you so much to everybody on our panel here today. I think it

51:33.880 --> 51:38.600
was a fantastic discussion. Thanks to everybody for watching, for submitting your questions. We are

51:38.600 --> 51:45.320
sorry if we didn't get to your question. But just a reminder, the recording will be sent out after

51:45.320 --> 51:53.000
this. So no need to worry about that. And yeah, once again, thanks, everybody. And we hope you

51:53.000 --> 52:10.280
can join us again in the future for another discussion like this. All right. Bye.
